# Git Commit Messages

## Workflow

- When asked for "commit text" or a commit message, **only output the text**. Do not run `git commit` unless the user explicitly asks you to commit.
- Base the message on the actual changes (`git status`, `git diff`, `git diff --staged`), not only on the conversation.
- If the change set includes files the user made separately (e.g. a file they added themselves), point that out under the message so they can unstage it if they want a separate commit.
- Never commit `.env`, secrets, or `package-lock.json` (this project uses Yarn 4).

## Format

Output two separate code blocks: one for the subject, one for the description.

### Subject

Follow [Conventional Commits](https://www.conventionalcommits.org/):

```
<type>(<scope>): <summary>
```

- `type`: `feat`, `fix`, `refactor`, `perf`, `test`, `docs`, `style`, `chore`, `build`, `ci`
- `scope`: the domain or area changed, lowercase (e.g. `auth`, `staff-users`, `products`, `orders`, `checkout`, `rules`)
- `summary`: imperative mood ("add", not "added"/"adds"), lowercase start, no trailing period, max 72 characters for the whole line
- Breaking changes: add `!` after the scope (`feat(auth)!: ...`) and a `BREAKING CHANGE:` paragraph in the description

### Description

- Start with one short paragraph saying what changed and why.
- Then group bullet points under plain-text headings that match the areas touched, in this order and only when relevant:
  - `Backend`: models, migrations, config, middleware, validators, repositories, services, transformers, controllers, routes, ace commands, seeders
  - `Frontend`: dependencies, providers, helpers, components, pages, layouts, styles
  - `Tests`
  - `Rules and tooling`: `.cursor/rules`, package/tooling changes
- Each bullet starts with an imperative verb (`Add`, `Replace`, `Remove`, `Rename`, `Switch`, `Fix`) and names the file, class or route in backticks only when it helps.
- Wrap lines at 72 characters.
- Mention migrations that require `node ace migration:fresh` or other manual steps in the description.

## Example

```
feat(auth): add separate staff User and Customer authentication
```

```
Replace the starter's single-user session auth with two isolated session
guards: `web` for back-office Users under /admin and `customer` for
storefront Customers under / and /account.

Backend
- Add Customer model and customers migration; switch users to UUID
  primary keys. Both models extend BaseAppModel.
- Configure `web` and `customer` session guards in config/auth.ts.
- Add single-action staff and customer controllers; login/signup/logout
  return JSON `{ data: { redirectTo } }`.

Frontend
- Add @tanstack/react-query and zod; wire QueryClientProvider.
- Add staff and customer auth pages with co-located _hooks.ts/_types.ts.

Rules and tooling
- Update Cursor rules to cover the two guards and UUID models.
```
